## Claim
Text: "Twitter now putting warnings on tweets that are just unedited clips of Biden saying he would end fracking"
Claim author: Zach Parkinson
Claim date: October 23, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### What specific Twitter policy (e.g., manipulated media, misleading information) was cited as the basis for placing warnings on tweets with clips of Joe Biden saying he would end fracking?
Answer: Based on the search result, the policy cited as the basis for placing warnings on tweets with clips of Joe Biden was Twitter's policy against **synthetic and manipulated media**, which prohibits users from "deceptively share synthetic or manipulated media that are likely to cause harm." The article notes that this policy covers "media altered in a more low-tech fashion" and that Twitter "will start labeling some tweets containing synthetic or manipulated media to provide more context."

Source URL: https://www.cnbc.com/2020/02/04/twitter-unveils-new-rules-to-tackle-deepfakes-ahead-of-2020-election.html

### Was the video clip of Joe Biden saying he would end fracking actually unedited, meaning it was a direct, untampered recording of his statement?
Answer: No, the video clip was not unedited; it was an abbreviated version of Biden's full response. According to FactCheck.org, the ad used a "clip from a July 2019 CNN debate" that showed Biden saying "we would make sure it’s eliminated," but his complete statement included "and no more subsidies for either one of those, either – any fossil fuel," meaning the clip was edited to omit part of his answer.

Source URL: https://www.factcheck.org/2020/06/misleading-ad-targets-biden-on-fossil-fuels-fracking/

### In what context did Joe Biden say he would end fracking (e.g., during a primary debate, a campaign speech, or an interview)?
Answer: Joe Biden's statement about ending fracking occurred during a March 2020 Democratic presidential candidate debate, where he appeared to agree with Bernie Sanders's call to end fracking, later clarifying his position as "No more—no new fracking."

Source URL: https://www.newsweek.com/video-resurfaces-kamala-harris-saying-she-wants-ban-fracking-after-saying-biden-will-not-during-1537304

### Did Twitter apply the warning to all tweets containing that specific clip, or only to certain accounts or posts?
Answer: Based on the article, Twitter applied the warning label to the specific tweet from White House social media director Dan Scavino and the subsequent retweet from Donald Trump's account, but it does not indicate whether the warning was applied to all tweets containing that clip or only to those particular posts. The article focuses on the actions taken on those accounts and does not mention any broader enforcement across other users sharing the same video.

Source URL: https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/social-media/facebook-twitter-put-warning-label-edited-video-biden-n1153506

### Did Joe Biden's statement about ending fracking appear in an unedited clip that was part of a longer speech or interview where he later clarified or altered his position?
Answer: Yes, according to the CNN fact-check, Joe Biden's statements about ending fracking were made during longer Democratic primary debates (e.g., July 2019 and March 2020) and were later clarified by his campaign. For instance, after the July 2019 debate, his campaign clarified that he "supports eliminating subsidies for coal and gas and deploying carbon capture," and after the March 2020 debate, they explained he was reiterating his plan to ban oil and gas permits on public land, not a complete ban on new fracking.

Source URL: https://www.cnn.com/2020/10/23/politics/biden-fracking-fact-check/index.html

